            <ArticleTitle>[Coincidence of coronary artery disease and takotsubo cardiomyopathy in a 72-year-old female patient].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>665-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s00063-008-1105-0</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Takotsubo cardiomyopathy is characterized by transient left ventricular dysfunction in patients with normal findings on coronary angiography. The simultaneous incidence of coronary vessel disease and takotsubo cardiomyopathy is described.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE REPORT" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 72-year-old, previously healthy female patient reported about chest pain. Acute emotional and physical stress were denied. Cardiac enzymes and electrocardiogram presented typical findings of myocardial infarction. Coronary angiography revealed a coronary one-vessel disease with occlusion of the second branch of the circumflex branch of the left coronary artery which did not explain the severe reduction of left ventricular function. After 4 months, left ventricular function and electrocardiogram had returned to normal.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Coronary artery disease does not rule out takotsubo cardiomyopathy.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
